1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,500
The mathematical heart of V-JEPA
is its prediction loss function.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:14,000
We compute the smooth L-1 distance between
the predicted latent features ŝ_k
and the target features s_k,

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
averaged over all M masked tokens.
This is fundamentally different
from pixel reconstruction —

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
the loss lives entirely in the abstract
semantic latent space.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Now watch the gradient flow
during backpropagation.
Gradients propagate backward from the loss
through the Predictor

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:39,500
and into the Context Encoder —
these two networks actively learn.

7
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:47,000
But when the gradient signal reaches
the Target Encoder boundary,
it hits a mathematical wall:
the stop-gradient operator.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:01:02,000
This barrier is absolutely essential.
Without it, the model could trivially
set both encoders to output the same constant —
making the loss zero
without learning anything useful.

9
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Without proper safeguards,
self-supervised learning falls into
a catastrophic failure mode called
Representation Collapse.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Imagine every possible video input —
a running athlete, a cooking tutorial,
a nature documentary —
being mapped to the exact same output vector.

11
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,500
The loss drops to zero,
yet the model has learned absolutely nothing.

12
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:33,500
V-JEPA escapes this trap through
two mathematically interlocked mechanisms.
The stop-gradient cuts the direct path.
The second mechanism is the
Exponential Moving Average.

13
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:43,000
Instead of training the Target Encoder directly,
its weights are updated as a slow,
momentum-weighted average
of the Context Encoder weights.

14
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The momentum m starts at 0.998
and gradually increases to 1.0 over training,
making the target encoder
progressively more stable.

15
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:05,000
When the Predictor achieves optimality
under the L-1 loss, the gradient signal
forces the Context Encoder to minimize
the Median Absolute Deviation of the targets.

16
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:22,000
This means the encoder is mathematically
obligated to capture rich, diverse representations —
collapse becomes provably impossible.

17
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Let us place V-JEPA on the
global benchmark leaderboard.

18
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:39,000
First, Something-Something-v2 —
the most demanding test of physical motion.
This dataset requires genuine understanding
of physical dynamics to succeed.

19
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,500
V-JEPA achieves 71.2 percent accuracy
under the fully frozen evaluation protocol.

20
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:56,000
This is a remarkable 10.0 percentage point
improvement over VideoMAE,
11 points over InternVideo,
21 points over DINOv2,

21
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:02,000
and a stunning 32 points over OpenCLIP.

22
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:14,000
The gap on SSv2 is the most telling result,
because large image-language models
that have seen billions of images
simply cannot understand physical motion.

23
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:28,000
On Kinetics-400, testing appearance and
context recognition, V-JEPA achieves 82.1 percent,
again surpassing all video self-supervised baselines.

24
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
For an absolutely fair comparison,
consider the controlled experiment.

25
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:44,000
We fix the architecture to identical
ViT-L/16 models and train both V-JEPA
and VideoMAE exclusively on Kinetics-400.

26
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Under this equal footing, V-JEPA still wins:
plus 0.7 points on K400,
plus 0.5 points on SSv2,
and plus 3.4 points on AVA action detection.

27
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:01,000
But the most striking result is data efficiency.
V-JEPA achieves this after seeing
just 210 million training samples.

28
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Compare to DINOv2 at 1,900 million — nine times more.
VideoMAEv2 needs 1,600 million.
OpenCLIP consumes 39,000 million —
nearly 200 times more than V-JEPA.

29
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:37,000
V-JEPA is not merely more accurate —
it is dramatically more sample-efficient
than any competing approach.

30
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
To measure representation quality
with maximum fairness, V-JEPA employs
the frozen evaluation protocol
with Attentive Probing.

31
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The entire V-JEPA video encoder —
hundreds of millions of parameters —
is completely locked.
Not a single backbone weight is updated.

32
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Instead, a lightweight cross-attention probe
is trained on top.

33
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:11,000
A single learnable query token attends
across all 1568 spatial-temporal encoder outputs,
intelligently pooling them into
one compact task representation.

34
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
This pooled vector then feeds
a simple linear classifier.

35
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Using this single frozen encoder,
V-JEPA achieves 77.9 percent on ImageNet-1K
with absolutely no image fine-tuning,

36
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:37,000
82 percent on Kinetics-400, 71 percent on SSv2,
and leading results across action localization,
scene recognition, and fine-grained identification —

37
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:45,000
all without modifying a single
backbone parameter.

38
00:05:45,000 --> 00:06:10,000
V-JEPA has proven the ultimate thesis:
when a machine learns to predict
the hidden latent world of video,
it simultaneously acquires the deepest
understanding of the entire visual universe.

39
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:30,000
V-JEPA
Video Joint Embedding Predictive Architecture
Meta AI Research · ICLR 2024
